   CSS NOTES: Pseudo-Class vs Pseudo-Element

   Pseudo-Class:
   - Styles an element based on its state or position
   - Applies to the entire element
   - Triggered by user interaction or condition
   - Uses single colon (:)

    Common Categories:
    1. User interaction:
        :hover, :active, :focus, :visited

    2. Form states:
        :enabled, :disabled, :checked, :required

    3. Structural positions:
        :first-child, :last-child,
        :first-of-type, :nth-child(),
        :nth-of-type()

    Syntax:
    element:pseudo-class

    Example:
    button:hover {
        background-color: blue;
   }----------------------------------

   Pseudo-Element:
   - Styles a specific part of an element
   - Applies to only part of the content
   - Does not depend on state
   - Uses double colon (::)

    It styles WHAT PART:
   - First line of text
   - First letter
   - Placeholder text
   - Selected text
   - Generated content

   Characteristics:
   - Applies to PART of an element
   - Does NOT depend on state
   - Always exists with the element
   - Uses double colon (::)

   Common Types:
   ::first-line
   ::first-letter
   ::before
   ::after
   ::placeholder
   ::selection

   Syntax:
   element::pseudo-element

   Example:
   p::first-letter {
     font-size: 32px;
   }
   ---------------------------------------------------------

   Key Difference:
   - Pseudo-class → WHEN / WHICH element
   - Pseudo-element → WHICH PART of the element

